      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To assess whether DNA methylation is associated with coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS & METHODS",
          "content": "An epigenome-wide analysis has been performed on leucocytes from familial hypercholesterolemic (FH) men with (n = 6) or without CAD (n = 6). The results were replicated in an extended sample of FH men (n = 61) and in non-FH men (n = 100) for two of the top differentially methylated loci."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "FH men with CAD had significantly more hypomethylated and hypermethylated loci and showed less DNA methylation level variability compared with men without CAD (p < 0.001). Moreover, COL14A1 and MMP9 DNA methylation levels were associated with CAD, age of onset of CAD or CAD risk factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These results suggest that epigenome-wide changes are associated with CAD occurrence in men."
        }
      ]},
